                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "35bf4518-7571-470a-a154-f1c98d5d0ef4",
                        "source_entry_id": "9261b065-e2b5-3c78-851d-bc44677499e1",
                        "source_sense_id": "38a0dd17-84ea-3c8d-accd-985b5acf1db1",
                        "target_lang_word": "nucleotide",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "nucleotide",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ff3d126f-dff2-3fea-a638-abcd7b17071c",
                                        "entry_id": "a2990c6e-69ff-37e4-b4af-ecd18beaad40",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The monomer constituting DNA or RNA biopolymer molecules. Each nucleotide consists of a nitrogenous heterocyclic base (or nucleobase), which can be either a double-ringed purine or a single-ringed pyrimidine; a five-carbon pentose sugar (deoxyribose in DNA or ribose in RNA); and a phosphate group."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e7d37f79-32d0-498a-b4f4-5a3f2a9f1abc",
                                                "sense_id": "ff3d126f-dff2-3fea-a638-abcd7b17071c",
                                                "text": "With a genome of only 400 million or so nucleotide bases, the pufferfish, Fugu rubripes rubripes, has the smallest known genome of any vertebrate animal. […] But the region of DNA between the landmarks can be a million or more nucleotides long."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c1666e13-1869-4af2-9c16-9e00332b2700",
                                                "sense_id": "ff3d126f-dff2-3fea-a638-abcd7b17071c",
                                                "text": "Magid draws […] a diagram depicting a restriction enzyme cutting neatly through a sequence of nucleotides."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "29039374-d4b1-434e-8ca0-254b55f2b9c0",
                        "source_entry_id": "9261b065-e2b5-3c78-851d-bc44677499e1",
                        "source_sense_id": "38a0dd17-84ea-3c8d-accd-985b5acf1db1",
                        "target_lang_word": "nucléotide",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "nucléotide",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "06f67b48-0c35-3372-8285-e9777859fcba",
                                        "entry_id": "0f491711-ec7c-3663-abfd-c77cb7f84a33",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Monomère, composant de base de l’acide désoxyribonucléique et de l’acide ribonucléique, composé d’un groupement phosphate (ou acide phosphorique), d’un pentose, sucre à cinq atomes de carbone (désoxyribose pour l’ADN et ribose pour l’ARN) et d’une base azotée (purine ou pyrimidine)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Génétique"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "92c18619-84e0-4261-9e0f-49d35757912e",
                                                "sense_id": "06f67b48-0c35-3372-8285-e9777859fcba",
                                                "text": "m représente le nucléotide à 2′-O-méthyler."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d9f4596c-8754-4070-8afc-8e7680ed18af",
                                                "sense_id": "06f67b48-0c35-3372-8285-e9777859fcba",
                                                "text": "l’ADN humain aligne trois milliards de nucléotides (le nom sous lequel on regroupe les molécules A, C, T et G), soit l'équivalent d’un texte de 750 000 pages ou encore 750 tomes d’une édition de la Pléiade."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d6d7fde4-2a44-43ae-bfed-df71d4896238",
                                                "sense_id": "06f67b48-0c35-3372-8285-e9777859fcba",
                                                "text": "Bienvenue à Gattaca : le terme Gattaca fait en fait référence aux quatre nucléotides qui composent l’ADN"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
